e('Almost every real-world encryption failure is a key-management failure. AES has never been broken in practice; AWS access keys committed to GitHub are found by bots within minutes.','Key handling is a major source of encryption failures, alongside implementation errors and protocol misuse. Publicly exposed credentials must be treated as compromised.\n\n> [!note] Content gap\n> A percentage of failures attributed to key management, a universal credential-discovery time and generic HSM throughput figures are omitted because no supporting measurement dataset was available.')
e('Every key goes through the same stages','A typical key lifecycle includes these stages')
e('**Store** so it cannot be copied:','**Store** with controls against unauthorised copying:')
e('| Source code / env var | Yes, trivially | Never for real secrets |','| Source code / env var | Readable by sufficiently privileged code | Avoid committed secrets; runtime injection still exposes the process |')
e('| Cloud KMS | No | Wrapping data keys |','| Cloud KMS | Usually not exported through normal key APIs; origin/protection level matters | Wrapping data keys |')
e('| HSM | No | CA roots, payment keys |','| HSM | Depends on key attributes and export policy | CA roots, payment keys |')
e('For keys that should never be seen by any application, you need hardware.','Hardware isolation can strengthen key custody; a software service can also hide key material from ordinary calling applications, with a different trusted boundary.')
e('Keys are generated inside, marked non-exportable, and never leave in plaintext.','Keys can be generated inside and marked non-exportable; supported import, backup and export policies vary. Properly configured non-exportable keys remain within the device\'s protected boundary during normal operations.')
e('- **Limited throughput**: typically hundreds to tens of thousands of operations per second, depending on the algorithm. That shapes architecture.','- **Limited throughput**: benchmark the specific device, algorithm, key size and concurrency before sizing a service.')
e('the hardware underneath every cloud KMS','hardware-backed tiers of cloud key-management services')
e('If an attacker compromises a server that is allowed to ask the HSM to sign, they cannot copy the key, but they can sign whatever they like for as long as they have access.','If an attacker compromises an authorised caller, a correctly enforced non-exportable key can resist extraction while the attacker still requests signatures permitted by its policy.')
e('**Cloud KMS** services (AWS KMS, Google Cloud KMS, Azure Key Vault) put HSM-backed keys behind an API with IAM permissions and audit logs.','**Cloud KMS** services expose key operations through APIs with access policies and audit facilities. Protection levels differ: Google Cloud KMS, for example, supports software, HSM and external protection; hardware backing is not universal.')
e('A KMS key never leaves the service.','For service-generated non-exportable keys, normal APIs return operation results rather than key material. Imported or externally managed keys have different custody boundaries.')
e('every call is checked against policy and logged (in AWS, to CloudTrail)','calls are checked against policy and can be audited (in AWS, through CloudTrail configuration)')
e('latency of milliseconds,','implementation-dependent latency,')
e('AWS KMS `Encrypt` accepts at most 4 KB of plaintext','AWS KMS symmetric `Encrypt` accepts at most 4,096 bytes of plaintext; RSA limits are smaller and depend on modulus and padding')
e('and wipe the plaintext DEK from memory.','and minimise the plaintext DEK\'s lifetime. Reliable erasure is runtime-dependent; the Python illustration below does not securely erase immutable byte objects.')
e('send the wrapped DEK (a couple of hundred bytes)','send the wrapped DEK (whose encoded size depends on the service)')
e('a ciphertext copied into another user\'s row will not decrypt','a ciphertext copied into another user\'s row will not decrypt when the application derives the expected context from trusted identity/row information')
e('it also appears in the audit log of every decrypt','it can appear in CloudTrail events, so it must not contain secret data')
e('only 32-byte keys do.','only key-sized values and request metadata do; wrapped keys include format overhead.')
e('to read any data you must call the KMS, which checks IAM and logs who decrypted what.','unwrap operations pass through KMS access controls and audit facilities. Cached plaintext DEKs allow subsequent reads without another KMS call, so an unwrap log is not a complete data-access log.')
e('rotating the KEK means re-wrapping small DEKs, not re-encrypting petabytes.','moving DEKs to a new uncompromised KEK can require only re-wrapping. Automatic version rotation may retain old wraps unchanged; compromise recovery can require fresh DEKs and data re-encryption.')
e('destroy a key and everything under it becomes unreadable, including copies in backups you cannot easily edit.','destroy every usable copy of the relevant keys and wrapped-key recovery paths to make remaining ciphertext unrecoverable, assuming sound encryption and no retained plaintext copies.')
e('and most database "transparent data encryption" work','and some database transparent-data-encryption designs work; details vary by service')
e('**KEK rotation**: AWS KMS keeps every previous version','**KEK rotation**: for AWS-generated symmetric encryption keys, AWS KMS keeps every previous version')
e('**Least privilege**: the order service may call `Decrypt` with context `service=orders`; analysts may not. Key administrators can manage keys but not use them.','**Least privilege**: configure policies so the order service may decrypt only its intended data/context and analysts may not. Administrative and cryptographic-use permissions can be separated; this is a policy design, not an automatic guarantee.')
e('because deleting a key deletes the data.','because loss of the last usable decryption key makes its ciphertext unrecoverable. It does not physically delete ciphertext or copies of plaintext.')
e('accepting a slightly larger exposure window.','accepting longer plaintext-key residency, weaker per-read audit granularity and delayed revocation. Cached keys help only until eviction or expiry; newly needed keys still require KMS.')
e('fails above 4 KB.','fails above the API\'s algorithm-specific payload limit (4,096 bytes for symmetric KMS encryption).')
e('most failures are in key handling, not the cipher.','secure key handling and correct protocol implementation both matter.')
e('HSMs generate and use keys that never leave tamper-resistant hardware; they prevent theft, not misuse by an authorised caller.','HSMs can generate and use non-exportable keys within tamper-resistant hardware; policy enforcement reduces extraction risk but authorised-call misuse remains possible.')
