f(1,'prompt','A developer stores an AES-256-GCM database key in config.yaml on the database server. An attacker obtains a shell as the application user with permission to read that file and database. What protection remains against this attacker?')
f(1,'workedExample','The attacker can obtain both ciphertext and key, so encryption does not protect those records. It can still protect a separately leaked backup that excludes the key. External KMS/HSM custody adds an access-control boundary, but a compromised application may retain permission to unwrap keys or decrypt; remote custody alone does not stop that misuse.')
f(2,'prompt','What is the intended benefit of an HSM configured to generate and use non-exportable keys?')
f(2,'explanation','Correct for correctly configured non-exportable keys under the device\'s supported security assumptions. HSMs may also support imported or exportable key objects, so attributes and policy matter.',0)
f(3,'workedExample','The DEK encrypts the file locally; the KEK wraps that DEK. KMS requests carry the small key or wrapped-key blob plus metadata, not the whole file. A 256-bit AES DEK is 32 bytes; its wrapped encoding is larger.')
f(4,'explanation','This ignores the nonce stored by seal() and its 16-byte tag. GCM requires a nonce input but does not inherently prepend it to ciphertext.',0)
f(6,'text','Bulk data stays local; small key material, wrapped-key blobs and request metadata cross the KMS boundary',1)
f(7,'explanation','Correct: 2^32 / 10^7 ≈ 429.5 days. Nonce reuse repeats the GCM encryption stream and undermines authentication; exact authentication-key recovery depends on the available messages and tags.',0)
f(7,'workedExample','For random 96-bit IVs, SP 800-38D limits total invocations under the key to 2^32. At 10 million/day this is 429.5 days. The birthday approximation q(q−1)/2^97 at q=2^32 is about 2^-33, below the 2^-32 design target. Reuse reveals overlapping plaintext XORs and can enable tag forgery. Fresh per-object keys reduce invocations per key; they do not excuse nonce reuse when an object key is reused.')
f(8,'workedExample','Automatic rotation protects new wraps, not old ciphertexts. A leaked old KEK can still unwrap captured old DEKs. Recovery requires assessing which DEKs were exposed, using fresh DEKs where needed and re-encrypting affected data; it cannot undo disclosure of captured old ciphertext. AWS-generated KMS material versions cannot be individually deleted: migrate away from the logical key before deleting it when appropriate.')
f(10,'prompt','Customer ciphertext remains in immutable backups. Which designs can make that ciphertext cryptographically unrecoverable, assuming all plaintext copies and every usable key recovery path are removed? Select all that apply. This is a technical question, not a legal determination of GDPR compliance.')
f(10,'explanation','Correct under the stated assumptions: all usable copies of the exclusive key, including recoverable wrapped copies, must be eliminated. This alone does not establish legal compliance.',0)
f(10,'workedExample','Crypto-shredding depends on losing every usable decryption path. A per-customer key can provide that boundary if it has no surviving copy. A separate mutable wrapped-key table can too, but deletion is not complete while its own backups can restore the key. Shared keys and automatic rotation preserve access to old ciphertext. Legal erasure requirements are not determined here because they depend on jurisdiction and processing context.')
f(11,'prompt','A hypothetical service repeatedly decrypts the same 100 DEKs through KMS and exceeds its request quota. Which change can reduce calls and tolerate brief outages for already cached keys, with an explicit security trade-off?')
f(11,'explanation','Correct when reuse produces cache hits. Caching increases plaintext-key residency, delays revocation and reduces per-read KMS audit granularity. It does not protect cache misses or survive beyond entry expiry.',0)
f(11,'explanation','Direct KMS encryption still adds remote calls and payload constraints; the exact call count depends on the redesigned request path.',3)
f(11,'workedExample','In one process with a stable 100-key working set, sufficient cache capacity and a 5-minute TTL, each key might need one unwrap per 5 minutes: about 100 calls instead of one per request. Multiple processes, eviction, expiry and cold starts increase that count. Cache keys must bind the encrypted key and relevant context, and enforce age/usage limits. AWS Encryption SDK support varies by language and component; verify the actual supported API.')
f(12,'workedExample','The root KSK signs root DNSKEY sets during controlled ceremonies, usually quarterly. Its compromise threatens the DNSSEC chain rooted in that trust anchor; it does not automatically decrypt traffic or override independently pinned trust anchors. Quorum controls, separate facilities and recorded procedures reduce insider risk and support auditability. A fixed multi-year recovery duration is omitted because incident replacement timing depends on circumstances.')
